.TH IMPROV 1 "" "improv" "Improv Manual"
.SH NAME
improv \- headless multidimensional spreadsheet model CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBimprov\fR [COMMAND]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B improv
is the command-line front-end to the Improv multidimensional spreadsheet.
It operates on a model store backed by an embedded (SQLite) Mentat datom
database, or, when
.I <db>
is a
.BI postgres:// ...?model= name
(or
.BR postgresql:// )
URL, on that model in a Postgres database's
.B improv
catalog. Each invocation opens the store, mutates the loaded model, and saves
it back.
.PP
A model is built from
.B categories
(dimensions such as Time or Product),
.B items
(members of a category, such as 2025 or WidgetA),
.B measures
(named variables indexed by a subset of the categories), and
.B input cells
(raw values at a coordinate). A coordinate maps category names to item names.
.PP
The
.B \-\-at
flag on
.B set
and
.B scenario
names a coordinate as
.IR Category = Item
pairs, resolved against the loaded model. Pairs may be comma-joined
.RB ( "\-\-at Time=Q1,Product=A" ),
space-separated after one flag
.RB ( "\-\-at Time=Q1 Product=A" ),
or spread over repeated flags
.RB ( "\-\-at Time=Q1 \-\-at Product=A" );
all are merged. Naming a category twice is an error, as is any argument that
is not a pair after
.BR \-\-at .
.PP
A command that takes a measure
.RB ( set ,
.BR show ,
.BR eval ,
.BR scenario ,
.BR save\-view ,
the refresh and export commands,
.BR stream )
takes its numeric id or its name:
.B improv eval model.db Revenue
is
.BR "improv eval model.db 102" .
An argument of all digits is an id. An unknown name is an error that lists
the measures, nearest first. The ids of new categories, items and measures
are given as numbers.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
improv\-init(1)
Create or open a store and install the schema
.TP
improv\-add\-category(1)
Add a category (a dimension, such as Time)
.TP
improv\-add\-item(1)
Add an item (a member of a category)
.TP
improv\-add\-measure(1)
Add an input measure
.TP
improv\-add\-derived(1)
Add a derived (formula) measure
.TP
improv\-define(1)
Create a measure or LAMBDA from a definition, "Target = ..."
.TP
improv\-register\-ext(1)
Register a Python external function
.TP
improv\-refresh\-ext(1)
Run a CALL(...) measure\*(Aqs function and store its cells
.TP
improv\-set(1)
Set an input cell
.TP
improv\-list(1)
Print the categories, items, measures, views and input cells
.TP
improv\-save\-view(1)
Save a named view of a measure, as the GUI does
.TP
improv\-show(1)
Print one measure and its input cells
.TP
improv\-eval(1)
Compute a derived measure and print its cells
.TP
improv\-scenario(1)
Add an input\-cell override to a what\-if scenario
.TP
improv\-export(1)
Print the whole model as JSON
.TP
improv\-import\-sql(1)
Import a SQLite query into a new SQL\-backed input measure
.TP
improv\-refresh\-sql(1)
Re\-run an SQL\-backed measure\*(Aqs query and replace its cells
.TP
improv\-refresh\-all(1)
Refresh every external measure now
.TP
improv\-serve\-refresh(1)
Refresh measures as their refresh policies fall due, until interrupted
.TP
improv\-export\-sql(1)
Write a measure\*(Aqs input cells to a SQLite table
.TP
improv\-import\-csv(1)
Import a CSV or TSV file into a new input measure
.TP
improv\-export\-csv(1)
Write a measure\*(Aqs cells to a CSV or TSV file
.TP
improv\-stream(1)
Apply input edits from stdin and print the cells they change
.SH FORMULAS
A derived measure's formula is an expression over measures, written
.BR "Target = expression" :
operators
.BR "+ - * / ^ & %" ,
comparisons
.BR "== <> < <= > >=" ,
.BR "AND OR NOT" ,
and 681 built-in functions with Excel's names
.RB ( ROUND ,
.BR PMT ,
.BR XLOOKUP ,
.BR NORM.DIST ,
\&...).
An aggregation reduces a measure along a category:
.BR "SUM(Sales OVER Month)" ;
.B WHERE
filters it, a window
.B OVER Month[FIRST..THIS]
makes a running total, and
.B INTO
lays a list result along a category.
.BR LET ,
.B LAMBDA
and named LAMBDAs
.RB ( "define db 1 \(dqMargin = LAMBDA(p, c, p - c)\(dq" )
are supported. Every function is listed, with its arguments, in the
Function Reference of the user guide (docs/src/functions.md); the language
is described in docs/src/formulas.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build a small Time \(mu Product model:
.PP
.RS
.nf
improv init          model.db
improv add-category  model.db 1 Time
improv add-category  model.db 2 Product
improv add-item      model.db 10 1 2025
improv add-item      model.db 20 2 WidgetA
improv add-measure   model.db 100 Price    number input
improv add-measure   model.db 101 Quantity number input
improv set           model.db 100 10 --at Product=WidgetA
improv set           model.db 101 100 --at Time=2025,Product=WidgetA
improv save-view     model.db 1 Prices 100 --rows Product --cols Time
improv list          model.db
improv show          model.db Price
improv export        model.db
.fi
.RE
Pipe new data through a model and print what it changes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
improv define model.db 102 \(dqRevenue = Price * Quantity\(dq
printf \(aqPrice 15 Product=WidgetA\en\(aq | improv stream model.db Revenue
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work on a model stored in Postgres:
.PP
.RS
.nf
improv list \(aqpostgres://me@127.0.0.1/sales?model=plan\(aq
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <db>
The model store: a single SQLite file created and read by
.BR improv ,
or a
.BI postgres:// ...?model= name
URL. An empty string names an in-memory database that is discarded on exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B IMPROV_LOCALE
The formula locale for
.B define
and
.BR add-derived ,
else the OS locale
.RB ( LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_MESSAGES ,
.BR LANG ).
In German
.RB ( de )
a formula may use the German function names, a decimal comma and
.B ;
between arguments:
.BR "WENN(Price > 1,5; RUNDEN(Price; 2); 0)" .
The English spelling is always accepted; models store it.
.TP
.B IMPROV_EXTFN_TRUSTED=1
Run external functions without the sandbox.
.TP
.B IMPROV_EXTFN_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED=1
Without bubblewrap, run external functions under resource limits only
(otherwise they are refused).
.TP
.B IMPROV_ENGINE_WORKERS
Threads one
.B eval
uses (1 to 64); by default one per 10,000 input cells, at most 8 and
the number of cores.
A FIXPOINT step over at least 20,000 rows reduces on as many threads.
The result is the same on any count.
.TP
.B MENTAT_MMAP_SIZE
Bytes of the store file SQLite may memory-map;
.B 0
turns mapping off.
